### Bridge-credit issuance, offer signatures, and intake hardening (2026-09-25)

- `BRIDGE_RELEASE`/`BRIDGE_REFUND` are reserved for internal issuance: REST and gossip intake refuse them (including `payload.type` smuggling through a `TRANSFER` envelope); only internal mempool paths emit them (`adf8991faf`). The withdraw monitor`s refund now debits the funding wallet via `TRANSFER` instead of a credit-type submission.
- v5 state-transition gates added: at `state_transition_v5_height` (24000) the pre-registered credit branch applies only to the matching bridge pseudo-address sender; other senders fall through to ordinary debit/balance/nonce validation. Sequential and pure/parallel paths share the predicate; pre-v5 blocks replay unchanged (`45ba8b5cb`).
- v5 bridge credits are now signature-bound, not just pseudo-sender-named: `confirm_transfer`/`refund_transfer` sign each credit's semantic fields (transfer id, chains, recipient, amount, credited value, proof, nonce, tx hash) with the bridge release key (`BRIDGE_RELEASE_PRIVATE_KEY`, falling back to `ESCROW_RELEASE_PRIVATE_KEY`), and consensus requires the signature to recover to the `bridge_release_authority` chain parameter (env fallback, then the escrow settlement authority). The proposer's pre-registered-row stamp branch enforces the same check so an unauthorized row is left un-sealed rather than committed to a block followers would reject; the parallel path stamps the existing row instead of inserting a duplicate and no longer overwrites the signed nonce. The refund row now stores `value=0` like the release row — the sender was already credited at issuance, so a nonzero value would double-credit at seal time.
- v6 refund lock binding: a `BRIDGE_REFUND` must name the sealed `BRIDGE_LOCK` it repays on the same chain via `payload.lock_tx_hash` (itself inside the signature projection), with matching lock sender and amount, and the lock must not already be refunded — even the authority key can then only anchor a refund for a real lock. Sequential and pure/parallel paths share `validate_bridge_refund_lock`; callers prefetch records via `build_bridge_lock_context`, which returns `None` when two refunds in one batch name the same lock so the block falls back to the ordered sequential path. Gated on `state_transition_v6_height` (env, 0 = disabled; `fleet-config-check.sh` watches `STATE_TRANSITION_V6_HEIGHT`); pre-v6 refunds replay lenient.
- `bridge_release_authority` is now an on-chain chain parameter (gap60-set-bridge-release-authority, sealed at block 22469), equal to the `ESCROW_RELEASE_ADDRESS` the RPC processes already sign with — the env fallback chain is no longer the source of truth.
- Unsigned `GPU_MARKET` offer transactions are no longer admitted anywhere: both intake doors require a secp256k1 signature recovering to `from`. The fee cap that bounded unsigned-offer nonce burn (`424bd917b`) is subsumed — unsigned offers are refused outright (`abbe67033a`).
- `SHOP_WALLET_ADDRESS` resolves to a file wallet by address so the unattended miner (`production_miner.py`) signs its listings; `aitbc market offer`/`cancel`/job paths fail fast when no key can sign for the configured sender.
- WebSocket lease refcount keyed by bound `node_id` (`0f0523560`).

### init_db authority seeding and config-checker repairs (2026-09-25)

- `init_db` now seeds `governance_executors`, `escrow_settlement_authority`, and `bond_slash_authority` on chains lacking them — donor-chain row first, env fallbacks (`GOVERNANCE_EXECUTORS`→escrow authority, `ESCROW_RELEASE_ADDRESS`, `BOND_SLASH_AUTHORITY_ADDRESS`) second — so a fresh chain is no longer born in the lenient pre-v5 executor branch. Rows carry `proposal_id="init_db-bootstrap"`; existing rows are never overwritten; unresolvable parameters warn loudly. `init_db()` also warns when it resolves to the `"ait-mainnet"` fixture fallback (`5bed50389`).
- `fleet-config-check.sh` now reads each unit's real `EnvironmentFiles` (fixing the node0 `ESCROW_RELEASE_ADDRESS` false flag), works under zsh remote shells (newline-safe loops replace word-splitting), reads protected env files via sudo fallback, distinguishes conflicting duplicate assignments (failure) from same-value ones (informational), and allowlists deliberate `unit:VAR` overrides such as the RPC unit's `ENABLE_BLOCK_PRODUCTION`/`BLOCK_PRODUCTION_CHAINS` layering.
- Fleet env hygiene: `HUB_DISCOVERY_URL` normalized to the bare hostname in `blockchain-secrets.env` on all five nodes; stale `MULTI_VALIDATOR_CONSENSUS_ENABLED=false`, node2's stray `PROPOSER_ID=0x4364`/`PROPOSER_KEY` pair in `blockchain-secrets.env`, sqlite `MEMPOOL_DB_URL` fallbacks, `ait-hub-island`, and bare-vs-`/rpc` `HUB_BLOCKCHAIN_RPC_URL` drift aligned to their effective values.
- `ait-mainnet` test-fixture chains (node0: 4649 non-contiguous blocks; node1: 179; node2: placeholder) moved off the `init_db` fallback path to `/var/lib/aitbc/archive/ait-mainnet/`.
- **Correction to the `init_db` entry above (`5bed50389`):** startup-time seeding of `chain_parameter` was reverted the same day (`e7ff9b4b`). Authority parameters are consensus state — writing them from node-local env or a donor copy lets nodes diverge silently (the state root only covers accounts) and applies the value to blocks replayed before it was legitimately set (`GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE` validates against the current table at every version). `init_db` now only warns when an authority parameter is unset; genesis and governance remain the only write paths.
- **Height-scoped authority chain parameters (`a2e339d41`, `de9c00d7e`, `84fa467b7`).** `chain_parameter` gained `applied_height` plus a `chain_parameter_history` table recording every change with its effective height. `governance_executors`, `escrow_settlement_authority`, `bond_slash_authority`, and `bridge_release_authority` resolvers now answer the value in force at the block being validated — the sender gate applies at every block version once set, so synced/replayed blocks must see the height-scoped value rather than the current row. A parameter set at height H resolves unset below H: replay can never fail because a value was set later in the chain. `validate_transaction`/`apply_transaction` take `block_height` through every caller (proposer, parent re-apply, sync import, escrow/bridge contexts — sequential and parallel paths identical). Genesis parameters take effect at height 0; `init_db` rebuilds the full history from sealed GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE payloads (live chain: executors@9819, escrow@22193, bond@22218, bridge@22469) and leaves operator-seeded rows NULL (status-quo always-apply semantics). Sync snapshot/delta payloads ship `applied_height` and the full history so followers preserve versioned values. Regression tests cover non-retroactivity, multiple changes, pre-executors replay, NULL semantics, genesis, and the sealed-execute rebuild.
